  <title>Switch wrappers</title>
  <p>A switch wrapper is a switch that another program runs: a <cmd>vde_switch</cmd> started by hand, by a script or by another project. The bricks of this project plug into it as into a switch of their own. Virtualbricks doesn't start it or stop it: the wrapper runs while a switch listens in its folder.</p>
  <figure>
    <title>The settings of a switch wrapper</title>
    <media type="image" mime="image/png" src="figures/switch-wrapper-settings.png">
      <p>The settings of wr1: Control folder, /tmp/lab-switch, with the button that chooses a folder beside it.</p>
    </media>
  </figure>
  <section id="switch">
    <title>Switch</title>
    <terms>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Control folder</gui></title>
        <p>The control folder of the switch: the folder that <cmd>vde_switch</cmd> makes for its sockets, the one its option <code>-s</code> names, with the socket <file>ctl</file> in it. Type it, or choose it with the button beside it. Without a folder the wrapper is <gui>Not configured</gui>.</p>
      </item>
    </terms>
    <p>For a switch started as</p>
    <screen>vde_switch -d -s /tmp/lab-switch</screen>
    <p>the control folder is <file>/tmp/lab-switch</file>.</p>
    <p>The ports of the switch, its hub mode and its spanning tree are the other program's: the wrapper has no settings of them.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="state">
    <title>Its state</title>
    <p>Virtualbricks looks at the folder every second:</p>
    <terms>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Running</gui></title>
        <p>A switch listens in the folder.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Stopped</gui></title>
        <p>The folder isn't there, as when the switch has quit and removed it.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Error</gui></title>
        <p>The folder is there, but no switch listens: it has no socket <file>ctl</file>, or the one that a killed switch leaves. The tooltip of the state says which, and so does the <gui>Logs</gui> window.</p>
      </item>
    </terms>
    <p><gui>Start</gui> only looks at the folder, and fails, saying why, when no switch listens there. <gui>Stop</gui> is refused while a switch listens: the program that runs it stops it. A brick that plugs into the wrapper starts only while its switch listens.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="running">
    <title>While it runs</title>
    <p>A new control folder takes effect at once: Virtualbricks looks at the new folder, and the bricks plugged into the wrapper join that switch the next time they start.</p>
  </section>
</page>
